Lab 1: Introduction to OpenCV for Computer Vision
Objective
Install OpenCV and gain hands-on experience with basic image processing tasks such as loading, displaying, saving, color conversion, and transformations using only your local Ubuntu machine.

Prerequisites
Start your Al Nafi-provided Ubuntu machine

Ensure Python 3.8+ is installed

Install OpenCV using pip:

pip install opencv-python
If you also want OpenCV with GUI support (cv2.imshow()), ensure the following package is installed:

sudo apt install python3-opencv -y
Steps
Step 1: Load and Display an Image
Create a working folder and script:

mkdir -p ~/opencv-lab
cd ~/opencv-lab
nano basic_opencv.py
Paste the following Python code:

import cv2

# Load an image
image = cv2.imread("sample.jpg")  # Make sure sample.jpg is in the same folder
if image is None:
    print("Image not found.")
    exit()

# Display the image
cv2.imshow("Original Image", image)
cv2.waitKey(0)
cv2.destroyAllWindows()
Add a sample image:

Copy any image into the ~/opencv-lab folder and rename it to sample.jpg, or

Download one offline via USB if needed.

Run the script:

python3 basic_opencv.py
Step 2: Save the Image
Extend your script to save a copy:

cv2.imwrite("output.jpg", image)
print("Image saved as output.jpg")
Step 3: Color Conversion and Transformations
Extend the script to include grayscale conversion and resizing:

# Convert to grayscale
gray = cv2.cvtColor(image, cv2.COLOR_BGR2GRAY)
cv2.imshow("Grayscale Image", gray)
cv2.imwrite("grayscale.jpg", gray)

# Resize the image
resized = cv2.resize(image, (200, 200))
cv2.imshow("Resized Image", resized)
cv2.imwrite("resized.jpg", resized)

cv2.waitKey(0)
cv2.destroyAllWindows()
Conclusion
In this lab, you:

Installed and configured OpenCV on your Ubuntu machine

Loaded, displayed, and saved images using Python

Performed basic transformations: grayscale conversion and resizing

This foundational practice introduces you to core computer vision workflows using OpenCV — without any cloud dependency.
